---
description: Project rules for the multilingual Next.js landing page (structure, tokens, i18n, interface feel, motion, editorial-tech design, changelog)
alwaysApply: true
---

## 1. Principles

- Prefer boring, explicit, readable code over clever code.
- Build the system first, then the pages. Every new value (color, size, duration) is a token before it is a class.
- Reuse before creating. Before writing a component, check whether an existing one can be extended through props or variants.
- Do not add a dependency without stating why and getting approval. Default to none. (`next-intl` is pre-approved.)
- Do not build features, pages, or abstractions that were not requested.
- If a requirement is unclear, ask. Do not fill gaps with invented content or design.
- Every component is written multilingual-ready and interface-feel-compliant from its first line. Neither is retrofitted.

## 2. Folder structure

```
messages/
  en.json                 # source of truth
  pt-PT.json              # must mirror en.json keys exactly
src/
  app/
    [locale]/
      layout.tsx          # <html lang>, fonts, NextIntlClientProvider, MotionProvider, shell
      page.tsx            # composes sections only; no markup logic
      not-found.tsx
    globals.css           # tailwind import, @theme tokens, base layer
    sitemap.ts            # includes per-locale alternates
    robots.ts
  i18n/
    routing.ts            # locales, defaultLocale, localePrefix
    request.ts            # getRequestConfig, loads messages
    navigation.ts         # localized Link, redirect, usePathname, useRouter
  proxy.ts                # locale negotiation (named middleware.ts on Next.js < 16)
  components/
    ui/                   # primitives: Button, Badge, Card, Heading, Text, Container, Icon,
                          # Eyebrow, DisplayHeading, SectionIndex, ImageCard, AvatarStack,
                          # Accordion, Carousel, VideoBackground...
    layout/               # Header, Footer, Nav, Section, LanguageSwitcher
    sections/             # Hero, Features, Pricing, FAQ, CTA... (one folder per section)
    motion/               # Reveal, Stagger, IconSwap, CountUp, Marquee, ScrollStack,
                          # presets, MotionProvider
  content/                # non-translatable structured data only (see section 5)
  config/
    site.ts               # name, url, nav structure (message keys), social links, metadata defaults
    motion.ts             # durations, easings, springs, stagger, distances
  lib/
    cn.ts                 # clsx + tailwind-merge
    utils.ts
  types/
    global.d.ts           # next-intl type augmentation (typed message keys)
public/
  brand/                  # logo, favicons
  images/
  video/                  # compressed hero/background video + poster frames
docs/
CHANGELOG.md
```

Rules:
- Use path alias `@/` for all imports. No deep relative imports (`../../../`).
- One component per file. File name matches the export, PascalCase. Folders are kebab-case.
- Section folders may contain private subcomponents that are only used by that section. Anything used by two or more sections moves to `components/ui` or `components/layout`.
- Barrel files (`index.ts`) only at `components/ui`. Nowhere else.
- Internal links and navigation always use `@/i18n/navigation`, never `next/link` or `next/navigation` directly.

## 3. Design tokens and theming

- All tokens live in one place: `@theme` in `globals.css` (Tailwind v4) or `tailwind.config.ts` (v3), backed by CSS variables.
- Two token layers:
  - **Primitive**: raw palette and scales (`--color-brand-500`, `--space-4`)
  - **Semantic**: purpose-based (`--color-bg`, `--color-surface`, `--color-text`, `--color-text-muted`, `--color-border`, `--color-accent`, `--color-accent-foreground`). Components consume semantic tokens only, never primitives.
- Dark mode (if in the design) is done by redefining semantic tokens under a `.dark` or `[data-theme]` selector. Components must not contain `dark:` overrides for colors. (The one exception is the image outline in section 7, which is defined as a semantic token so it also needs no override.)
- Tokens to define at minimum: colors, font families, type scale (size + line-height + tracking), spacing/section rhythm, container widths, radii, shadows (layered, see section 7), z-index scale, breakpoints, motion durations, easings, springs, stagger delay, press scale, minimum hit area.
- Forbidden in components: arbitrary values like `text-[17px]`, `bg-[#1a1a1a]`, `mt-[37px]`, raw hex/rgb/hsl, magic z-index numbers. If a needed value is not a token, add the token first.
- Fonts via `next/font` with `display: swap`, exposed as CSS variables mapped to the theme. Confirm the font subset includes Portuguese glyphs (ã, õ, ç, á, é, í, ó, ú, â, ê, ô, à).

## 4. Components

- Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` only to the smallest leaf that needs state, effects, or motion. Never at a section root if avoidable.
- Variants via `class-variance-authority` (cva) only if approved as a dependency; otherwise a typed variant map object. No long ternary chains for class names.
- Merge classes with `cn()` (clsx + tailwind-merge). Every UI primitive accepts and merges `className`.
- Props: explicit TypeScript interfaces, exported when reused. Extend native element props (`React.ComponentProps<"button">`) instead of redefining them. Use `React.ReactNode` for slots. Avoid boolean prop explosions; prefer a `variant` or `size` union.
- UI primitives are language-agnostic: they never import translations. They receive text through `children` or props. Sections own the translation calls.
- Polymorphism: use `asChild` or an `as` prop only where truly needed (e.g. Button rendering as a link).
- Composition over configuration: `<Card><Card.Header/>...</Card>` or plain children, not 15 props.
- Semantic HTML first: `header`, `nav`, `main`, `section` with `aria-labelledby`, `footer`, `ul/li` for lists, `button` vs `a` used correctly. One `h1` per page, heading levels never skipped.
- A `Section` layout component owns vertical rhythm, container width, and the anchor `id`. Sections must not re-implement padding or max-width.
- Do not create wrapper components that add nothing.
- Interactive primitives (Button, links, toggles, inputs) ship with all states designed from the start: default, hover, focus-visible, active/pressed, disabled, and loading where relevant.

## 5. Internationalization and content

**Setup**
- Library: `next-intl`, App Router integration, `[locale]` segment.
- Locales: `["en", "pt-PT"]`, `defaultLocale: "en"`. Use `localePrefix: "as-needed"` (English at `/`, Portuguese under its prefix) unless we agree otherwise in Phase 0.
- `setRequestLocale(locale)` in every layout and page, plus `generateStaticParams`, so pages stay statically rendered.
- `<html lang={locale}>` set in the locale layout. Validate the incoming locale and call `notFound()` for unknown ones.

**Messages**
- All user-visible text lives in `messages/{locale}.json`. No literal strings in JSX, `aria-label`, `alt`, `title`, placeholders, or metadata.
- Structure keys by section, semantic not text-derived: `hero.title`, `pricing.plans.pro.cta`. Never `hero.buildFasterToday`.
- `en.json` is the source of truth. Augment next-intl types so keys and rich-text tags are type-checked. `pt-PT.json` must match its shape exactly. Add a `check:i18n` script that fails when keys are missing or extra, and include it in `check`.
- Never concatenate translated fragments to build a sentence. Use ICU placeholders (`{name}`), ICU plurals, and `t.rich()` for inline links and emphasis. Word order differs between languages.
- Server Components use `getTranslations`. Client Components use `useTranslations` and only receive the messages namespace they need through the provider.
- Numbers, dates, and currencies go through `useFormatter` / `getFormatter`, never hand-formatted.

**Portuguese (Portugal) rules**
- Use European Portuguese vocabulary and spelling: *ecrã* (not tela), *utilizador* (not usuário), *telemóvel* (not celular), *ficheiro* (not arquivo), *equipa* (not equipe), *registar/registo*, *subscrição*, *contacto*. Use the "tu"/"você"/impersonal register decided in Phase 0 consistently.
- If final `pt-PT` copy exists in `/docs/copy`, use it verbatim. If not, draft it, and list every drafted key under **Known issues** in the changelog as "needs native review". Never present a machine draft as final.
- Portuguese runs roughly 15 to 30 percent longer than English. No fixed widths or heights on text containers, no truncation to hide overflow. Test every section in both languages.

**Routing, SEO, and switching**
- Locale detection in `proxy.ts` (`middleware.ts` before Next.js 16) via `next-intl`, with the preference persisted in its cookie.
- `generateMetadata` per locale: translated title, description, Open Graph, Twitter, `og:locale` (`en_US`, `pt_PT`).
- `alternates.languages` with `hreflang` for `en`, `pt-PT`, and `x-default` (pointing to English), plus a self-referencing canonical per locale.
- `sitemap.ts` lists every locale URL with its alternates. JSON-LD uses `inLanguage`.
- `LanguageSwitcher` in the header and footer: shows language names in their own language (`English`, `Português`), never flags, since flags represent countries, not languages. Each option has the correct `lang` and `hreflang`, keeps the current path and hash, is keyboard accessible, and announces the current language.
- Use logical Tailwind properties (`ms-*`, `me-*`, `ps-*`, `pe-*`, `start-*`, `end-*`, `text-start`) instead of left/right where direction could matter, to keep future RTL languages possible.

**Non-translatable content**
- `src/content/*.ts` only holds structured non-text data: image paths, icon names, link targets, numeric values, ordering. Text is referenced by message key.
- Repeated items render with `.map()` over typed arrays with stable keys (never array index for reorderable data).
- Global values (site name, URLs, nav structure, socials) come from `config/site.ts`.
- No lorem ipsum or placeholder text in final code. If copy is missing, leave a clearly marked key and flag it in the reply.

## 6. Styling (Tailwind)

- Mobile-first: base styles are mobile, then `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`.
- Use the token-backed utilities (`text-muted`, `bg-surface`, `rounded-lg`) not one-off values.
- Class order is enforced by `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss`.
- If the same class string appears 3+ times, extract a component or variant. Do not use `@apply` except in the base layer for global elements.
- Fluid type via `clamp()` defined inside the type-scale tokens, not scattered in components.
- Layout with grid/flex and gap. No margin hacks, no fixed heights on content containers, no `!important`.
- Express every polish detail in Tailwind. Never introduce a second styling system.

## 7. Interface feel (make-interfaces-feel-better)

Source: https://github.com/jakubkrehel/make-interfaces-feel-better. If the skill is installed, load and follow it. The following is the condensed, mandatory version. These are requirements for every component at creation time.

**Surfaces**
- **Concentric radius**: outer radius = inner radius + padding. Define radius tokens so nesting is computed, not eyeballed. Never use the same radius on a parent and its padded child.
- **Shadows for elevation, borders for structure**: cards, buttons, and containers whose border only fakes depth use layered, transparent `box-shadow` tokens. Keep real borders for dividers, layout separation, and selected/focus states.
- **Image outlines**: images get a `1px` outline, `outline-offset: -1px`, in **pure** black at 10% in light mode (`oklch(0 0 0 / 0.1)`) and **pure** white at 10% in dark mode (`oklch(1 0 0 / 0.1)`). Never a tinted neutral (slate, zinc). Defined once as a semantic token.
- **Hit areas**: interactive elements are at least 44×44px on touch, and never below 40×40px on dense desktop UI. If the visible element is smaller, extend with a pseudo-element. Hit areas of neighbouring elements must never overlap.
- **Optical alignment**: when geometric centering looks off (icon + label buttons, play triangles, asymmetric icons), align optically with padding or by fixing the SVG. Icon-adjacent padding is usually slightly smaller on the icon side.

**Typography**
- `-webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased` on the root (`antialiased` in Tailwind).
- `text-wrap: balance` on headings, `text-wrap: pretty` on body text. Verify in both languages.
- `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` (`tabular-nums`) on any number that updates or aligns in columns (counters, prices, stats, timers).

**Animations**
- **Interruptible by default**: interactive state changes (hover, focus, press, toggles) use CSS transitions, which can be interrupted mid-flight. Keyframes only for staged sequences that run once.
- **Never `transition: all`.** Always list exact properties (`transition-transform`, `transition-[opacity,scale]`).
- **Scale on press**: `scale(0.96)`, always. Never below `0.95`. Buttons accept a `static` prop to disable it. Value comes from a token.
- **Enters**: split into semantic chunks and stagger by about 100ms **only** for infrequent staged entrances (hero, section reveals). Never stagger routine or high-frequency interactions. Use `ease-out`.
- **Exits**: softer than enters. Small fixed `translateY`, never full height. `ease-out`.
- **Icon swaps** (copy → check, menu → close, theme toggle): animate `opacity` 0→1, `scale` 0.25→1, `blur` 4px→0px, exactly those values. With Motion: `transition: { type: "spring", duration: 0.3, bounce: 0 }`. Bounce is always `0`. Without Motion: keep both icons in the DOM, one absolutely positioned, and cross-fade with `cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1)`. Ship this as the reusable `IconSwap` component.
- **Skip animation on load**: `initial={false}` on `AnimatePresence`. Intentional entrance animations (e.g. hero) are the explicit exception and must be verified.
- **`will-change`**: only `transform`, `opacity`, `filter`, only when first-frame stutter is observed. Never `will-change: all`.
- **Motion restraint**: no custom animation on high-frequency interactions (hover on lists, typing, repeated clicks). Use instant feedback or an opacity/color transition of 150ms or less. Motion is never the only feedback: every animated change also has a static cue (color, icon, or label).

**Icons**
- One icon set only. Never mix libraries on one surface.
- Stroke `1.5px` beside regular (400) text, `2px` beside semibold (600).
- One SVG per icon using `currentColor`. Hover/selected/disabled states come from CSS color and opacity, never from separate assets.
- Outline is the default variant. Fill marks the active or selected state.
- Directional icons (arrows, chevrons) must flip correctly in RTL via logical utilities.

**Review process**
- After every section: run a `quick` review (primary path and highest-traffic states, HIGH and MEDIUM only) and fix everything found before moving on.
- Phase 3: run a `full` review across typography, surfaces, animations, icons, and performance.
- Walk every state (hover, focus, active, loading, empty) and replay motion at 10% speed in the browser's Animations panel. Whatever feels off at 10% is subtly wrong at full speed.
- Report the verdict (`Approve` / `Needs changes` / `Block`), what was fixed, considered-but-rejected candidates, and anything **Not verified**. Record it in the changelog.

## 8. Motion (Motion library)

- Import from `motion/react`.
- Wrap the app in one `MotionProvider` using `LazyMotion` with `domAnimation` and use the `m` component to keep the bundle small. Set `MotionConfig reducedMotion="user"`.
- All durations, easings, springs, distances, and stagger values come from `config/motion.ts`. No inline magic numbers in components.
- Build a small set of reusable primitives (`Reveal`, `Stagger`, `IconSwap`, etc.) and use them. Sections do not hand-roll `initial/animate/transition`.
- Use Motion for enter/exit and orchestrated sequences. Use CSS transitions for hover, focus, and press (see section 7).
- Animate `transform`, `opacity`, and `filter` only. Never animate layout properties (width, height, top, left) unless via the `layout` prop with a reason.
- Scroll reveals use `whileInView` with `viewport={{ once: true, margin: ... }}` and are applied to section-level chunks, not to every element on the page.
- Above-the-fold animation is subtle and short. It must not delay LCP or cause layout shift.
- Every animation degrades to static content under `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Language switching must not replay entrance animations or cause layout jumps.

## 9. TypeScript

- `strict: true`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true`.
- No `any`, no `as` casts to silence errors, no `@ts-ignore`. Use `unknown` and narrow, or fix the type.
- Prefer `type` for unions and props, `interface` when extending. Use `satisfies` for typed config/content objects.
- Export types from where they are defined. No duplicated type declarations.
- No enums; use string literal unions or `as const` objects.
- Locale type derives from `routing.locales`, never redeclared.

## 10. Accessibility

- Color contrast meets WCAG AA at minimum for all token pairings; verify the semantic token pairs.
- Visible, consistent `:focus-visible` styles from a token.
- All interactive elements are reachable and operable by keyboard.
- Images: meaningful, translated `alt`, or `alt=""` for decorative. Decorative SVGs get `aria-hidden`.
- Add a translated "Skip to content" link.
- Mobile nav: focus trap, `Escape` to close, correct `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`.
- No information conveyed by color alone or by motion alone.
- Any text in another language inside a page (e.g. the language names in the switcher) carries the right `lang` attribute.

## 11. Performance and SEO

- `next/image` for every raster image with explicit dimensions or `fill` + `sizes`. Priority only on the LCP image.
- SVG logo/icons inlined or via a single icon system. Icon library imports are per-icon, never the whole package.
- No layout shift: reserve space for images, fonts, animated elements, and longer translated text.
- Metadata through the App Router Metadata API (see section 5 for locale handling): title template, description, canonical, Open Graph, Twitter, icons, `metadataBase`. JSON-LD via a small typed component.
- `sitemap.ts` and `robots.ts` generated.
- Send only the messages a client component needs. Do not ship the full message file to the browser.
- Avoid client-side data fetching and heavy third-party scripts. Load any analytics with `next/script` and a non-blocking strategy.

## 12. Code quality

- No dead code, no commented-out blocks, no unused exports or imports, no `console.log`.
- Comments explain *why*, never *what*. No comments that narrate the code.
- Names are specific: `PricingCard`, not `Card2`; `isMenuOpen`, not `flag`.
- Functions and components stay small; if a component exceeds about 120 lines, split it.
- Env vars validated and typed in one place if any are used. No secrets in the client bundle.
- ESLint (`next/core-web-vitals`, `typescript-eslint`) and Prettier configured and passing. Add `typecheck`, `check:i18n`, and a `check` script that runs lint, typecheck, i18n check, and build.
- Commit-ready state: every reply that changes code leaves the project building.

## 13. Communication rules for the AI

- Before large changes, state the plan in a few lines.
- After changes, report: files created/modified, new tokens, new message keys, new dependencies, deviations from these rules with reasons, and open questions.
- Never silently change design tokens or copy.
- Never add a string in one language without adding it in the other in the same change.
- If a rule here conflicts with a request, say so and ask before proceeding.

## 14. Editorial-tech design language and inspiration patterns

**Reference sites** (style references only; see precedence below):

| Site | Patterns worth studying |
| --- | --- |
| Farmio, https://farmio.framer.website/ | Video/background hero with eyebrow and oversized headline; stat row; sticky stacked services with a `01/05` counter and a bracketed scroll hint; three-step "how it works"; mixed-ratio gallery; carousel testimonials; accordion FAQ; full-bleed CTA; large footer |
| GreenRoots, https://greenroots-agriculture.framer.website/ | Giant stacked wordmark over a video hero; uppercase eyebrow labels; three-stat row; services as a link list; three-tier pricing; testimonial beside a large image; quote form; newsletter footer with an oversized wordmark and back-to-top |
| Agronex, https://agronex.framer.website/ | Rounded full-width hero image; tall portrait image cards in a row; numbered benefits `01` to `03`; big-number impact stats; testimonial slider with image; newsletter footer |
| Verdano, https://verdano.framer.website/#services | Video hero with avatar-stack social proof (count and rating); animated stat numbers; partner logo marquee; mixed-aspect services grid; feature accordion; pricing with a billing-period toggle; large quote testimonial; contact form beside an image |

**Shared design language** to carry into our design (using our tokens, not theirs):
- Oversized display headlines with tight tracking, always paired with a small eyebrow label above (uppercase or bracketed) and often a numeric index (`01/05`)
- Big numerals for stats, with a short caption beneath
- Large-radius image cards, portrait and mixed aspect ratios, full-bleed imagery, one video hero
- Generous whitespace, a strong and consistent vertical rhythm, one accent color, pill-shaped buttons
- Scroll-linked storytelling used sparingly: a sticky stack, a marquee, count-up numbers
- An oversized footer wordmark closing the page

**Precedence and originality**
- Order of authority: our design variables and copy, then these project rules and the interface-feel standard (section 7), then the references. The references never override the first two.
- Never reproduce a reference layout pixel for pixel, or reuse its copy, imagery, video, icons, or branding. Recombine the patterns into our own composition.
- Do not derive colors, fonts, or spacing from the references. Those come from `/docs/design`.
- Only build sections that exist in our copy. If our content has no pricing, we build no pricing.

**Pattern to component mapping and constraints**

- `Eyebrow`, `DisplayHeading`, `SectionIndex`: display sizes come from fluid `clamp()` type tokens. Headings use `text-wrap: balance`, and `hyphens: auto` (which needs the correct `lang`) so long Portuguese words do not overflow at large sizes. Test every display heading in both languages at 320px and 1920px. The index and any bracketed hint (e.g. "[ Keep Scrolling ]") are translated strings, and the bracket style is CSS or a fixed component detail, not part of the message.
- `CountUp` (stats): animates once when it enters view. Final value is in the DOM as real text and readable by assistive tech. Uses `tabular-nums` so digits do not shift. Values and suffixes (`K+`, `%`, `B`) are formatted with the locale formatter. Shows the final value immediately under reduced motion. No layout shift while counting.
- `Marquee` (logos, ticker): CSS-driven `transform` loop. The duplicated track is `aria-hidden`. Pauses on hover and focus, and is static under reduced motion. Logos have accessible names. Do not put essential information only inside a moving element.
- `ScrollStack` (sticky stacked services or benefits): built on native `position: sticky` with Motion `useScroll` and `useTransform` driving `transform` and `opacity` only. Never hijack or lock native scrolling. Content is fully readable and correctly ordered without JS, on mobile, and under reduced motion, where it collapses to a normal vertical list. The counter is derived from scroll state. At most one scroll-linked signature moment per page unless approved in Phase 0.
- `Accordion` (FAQ, feature list): keyboard operable with correct `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, or native `<details>`. One consistent open/close animation of 200ms or less from tokens. The answer text is in the DOM for SEO. FAQ content also emitted as `FAQPage` JSON-LD per locale.
- `Carousel` (testimonials): scroll-snap based, keyboard and swipe operable, visible previous/next buttons with translated accessible labels, and a position indicator. No autoplay by default, and if added it must be pausable and off under reduced motion.
- `ImageCard` and gallery: mixed aspect ratios via tokens, `next/image` with `sizes`, concentric radius when nested, and the image outline from section 7. Reserve space so nothing shifts.
- `VideoBackground` (hero): `muted`, `loop`, `playsInline`, no audio track, with a poster. The poster is the LCP element (`next/image`, `priority`), and the video loads after it (`preload="metadata"` or lazy attach) and pauses when off-screen. Under reduced motion or data-saver, show only the poster. Ship compressed WebM/MP4 and agree a size budget in Phase 0. Only one video above the fold, and text over media must meet AA contrast using a token-based scrim.
- `AvatarStack` (social proof): decorative avatars get `alt=""`. The count and rating are real text and translated. Do not invent numbers, ratings, or testimonials. Use only what exists in `/docs/copy`.
- `PricingToggle` (billing period), only if pricing exists in the copy: accessible switch or radio group, a static cue for the active state, prices and currency through the formatter, and `tabular-nums`.
- Oversized footer wordmark: use the logo SVG or fluid text sized with `clamp()`. If it duplicates the visible logo it is `aria-hidden`. It must never cause horizontal overflow, in either language.

**Motion budget** (the references animate heavily, we do not):
- Allowed by default: one hero entrance, one scroll-linked signature moment, count-up on stats, and at most one marquee.
- Everything else uses static layout or short CSS transitions, per the motion restraint rule in section 7.
- Anything beyond this budget needs approval in Phase 0 and a reason recorded in the changelog.

**Media performance guardrails**
- Every image through `next/image` in AVIF/WebP with `sizes`. Only the LCP image is `priority`.
- No autoplaying media that blocks LCP, no layout shift from late-loading media, and no third-party embeds without approval.
- Test on a throttled mobile connection. If the video or scroll effects hurt Lighthouse Performance below the target, cut the effect, not the target.

## Changelog rule

Maintain `/CHANGELOG.md` at the repo root. It reads like an **end-of-day report to a project lead**: what moved forward, what was decided, and what needs attention. It is not an edit log. Git history already records every individual change.

### One entry per day, showing the final state

- There is **one entry per working day**, headed by the date. The first change of the day creates it. Every later change that day **edits that same entry**; it never adds a second one.
- Record the **net result at the end of the day**, not the steps taken to get there:
  - Something changed several times (a font size tuned 10 times) is one bullet describing where it ended up.
  - Something added and then removed on the same day does not appear at all.
  - A revert is not an item. Reword the original bullet or delete it.
  - A later fix to something built the same day goes into that thing's bullet, not into a separate "Fixed" line.
- Before touching the entry, re-read today's entry and merge into the existing bullets. Add a new bullet only for a genuinely new outcome.
- Entries from past days are final. Do not rewrite them. If something from a past day changes, report the new state in today's entry.

### What is worth a bullet

Write about outcomes a lead would care about: a section or page shipped, a visible design change, a new behaviour, a decision, a new dependency, a problem found or solved, an open blocker. Group related work under one bullet ("Header: clear over the hero, hides on scroll down, returns solid on scroll up") instead of listing each tweak.

Leave out: file lists, class names, pixel-level tuning steps, typo or formatting fixes, and anything the reader cannot act on. Mention a file, token, or key only when a future developer really needs it (for example a new token family or a placeholder that must be replaced).

### Format

Newest day at the top, under `## [Unreleased]`. Date format `YYYY-MM-DD`. Sections with nothing to say are **omitted**, not filled with `(none)`. Aim for something that fits on one screen; a heavy day might need 25 bullets, a light day 3.

```md
## [Unreleased]

### 2026-10-01

**Summary**
Two to four plain sentences: what moved forward today and where the project stands now.

**Done**
- **Hero:** full-screen photograph with a light scrim; headline on the display scale with an italic accent word.
- **Header:** clear over the hero on the landing page, hides on scroll down, comes back solid on scroll up.
- **Design system:** Inter for body text; the page base is now white and the footer sits on Deep Forest.

**Decisions**
- Inter for body text departs from the moodboard (which names Sora); chosen for readability at long lengths.

**Needs attention**
- Portuguese copy is a machine draft and needs native review (all `pt-PT` keys added today).
- Missing assets: partner logos, Spain Living Lab photo.
- Not verified: Lighthouse on a production host.

**Technical notes**
- Dependencies: added `lenis` (smooth scrolling, approved).
- Tokens: new `--font-body`, inverse colour set (`--color-bg-inverse`, ...); removed `--color-scrim`.
- Messages: new `livingLabs.labs.*.imageAlt`; removed `footer.wordmark`.

**Checks**
typecheck pass · lint pass · i18n pass · build pass · checked at 390 / 1440 in en and pt-PT
```

### Rules for entries

- Plain language, complete sentences, specific outcomes. Never "various improvements".
- **Always reported, even when small:** dependencies added, removed, or upgraded; the net set of tokens and message-key groups added or removed; deviations from these rules or the design docs, with the reason; motion-budget exceptions.
- Every machine-drafted or unreviewed translation stays listed under **Needs attention** in each day's entry until a human confirms it.
- Interface-feel reviews appear as one line under **Done** (mode, verdict, what was fixed) and anything not verified goes under **Needs attention**.
- **Checks** is the state at the end of the day, not a history of runs. If a check was not re-run after the last change, say so.
- When a milestone is complete (e.g. Phase 1 done, launch), convert `[Unreleased]` into a version heading like `## [0.1.0] - YYYY-MM-DD` and start a fresh `[Unreleased]`.
- The final message of every major task must end with: `Changelog updated: <YYYY-MM-DD> report`.
